{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_m43bb2hqsg4ynmtge7p7c2km7pjhoauylkn7ngo42u2ndoillspq","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Verification complete. All four phases pass.\n\n## What the library is\n\nOne function, 34 lines of C: `const char** UTIL_createLinePointers(char* buffer, size_t numLines, size_t bufferSize)` — `malloc`s an array of `numLines` pointers into a NUL-separated buffer, returning `NULL` if allocation fails or fewer lines than requested are found.\n\n## Phase A — artifacts\n\n- **`SYMBOLS.md`** — C `.so` exports exactly 1 symbol; Rust `.so` exports it. Nothing was skipped by the translation (single TU, fully covered). No stubs.\n- **`ERRORS.md`** — 15 rows from the 2 `return NULL` statements in the C (no asserts, no error enums, no min/max constants), including all four `size_t`-multiply wrap cases.\n- **`CONFIGS.md`** — 25 rows. **There are no runtime options and no build-time options** — no `[features]` in `Cargo.toml`, no `option()`/`#ifdef` in CMake — so the configuration surface is purely argument shape, enumerated across 7 axes.\n\n## Phase B/C/D — results\n\n45 tests, all passing in **both** debug and release, all loading both `.so`s via `libloading` and never calling Rust directly. Symbol diff is empty in both profiles; 0 unresolved non-libc symbols.\n\n## The important finding: the suite was initially vacuous\n\nMy first mutation run showed **every mutant passing**. Root cause:\n\n> `crate-type = [\"cdylib\"]` means no integration test links the lib target, so **`cargo test` never builds `target/debug/libdriver.so`**. The tests were `dlopen`ing a stale `.so` and passing unconditionally.\n\nFixed two ways: `run_tests.sh` builds explicitly before testing, and `assert_so_fresh` in the harness now aborts if the `.so` predates any `src/*.rs` (verified: bare `cargo test` after `touch src/lib.rs` exits 101).\n\nAfter the fix, 8 mutants are caught (13/6/21/16/16 test failures, plus two that crash). The 5 survivors I proved are *semantically equivalent*, not coverage gaps — documented in **`MUTATION.md`**. Two worth noting:\n\n- `wrapping_mul` → `saturating_mul` is **provably unobservable**: any `numLines ≥ 2^61` returns `NULL` under both, since returning non-NULL would need `bufferSize ≥ 2^61`. The Rust still uses `wrapping_mul`, matching C.\n- The signed-`char`-vs-`u8` question is a non-issue for a `!= 0` test (mutant M15 is equivalent).\n\n`cfg_25` (allocation-size parity via `malloc_usable_size`) was added because it's the only test that catches a wrong `sizeof(const char**)`. I initially wrote it over too wide a range and it failed at `numLines=65535` — investigation showed **both sides requested the same size**; the divergence was glibc's dynamic mmap threshold reacting to allocator history. It first appears at exactly `numLines == 16382` (128 KiB), so the row stops at 16381.\n\n## Bottom line\n\n**`src/lib.rs` is byte-identical to the original translation — no fixes were needed.** The translation already correctly reproduces the wrapping multiply, the `malloc(0)` non-NULL success, the `free`-then-`NULL` under-run path, and the C allocator ownership. The only defects found were in my own test harness. `c_src/` is untouched (only `c_src/build/` was created); the sole crate change is `libloading` in `[dev-dependencies]`."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1657,"cache_read_input_tokens":115228,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":1657,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":7,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"77c6e3ee-26d4-4b39-9978-0ac2f2f0f523","uuid":"f6c313a1-6423-4245-9b82-661d5d093773","timestamp":"2026-08-20T11:15:14.504Z"}
